---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
import { local } from '../lib/urls';
---
<BaseLayout title="About — Abdelrahman Elsayed" description="How I approach electronics, software, and connected systems, and a little about the person behind the projects." active="about">
  <header class="page-heading about-heading"><p class="eyebrow">The person behind the projects</p><h1>A little about me.</h1></header>
  <section class="about-opening" aria-label="Introduction">
    <div class="about-words" aria-hidden="true"><span>Hardware.</span><span>Software.</span><span>People.</span></div>
    <div class="about-narrative"><p class="about-introduction">I’m Abdelrahman. My background is in electronics and communications, and I’m based in Montreal. I’m curious about how individual pieces become a working system.</p><p>That curiosity has taken me from sensors and embedded prototypes to communication networks and industrial research. I enjoy moving between the physical device, the software around it, and the connections that let the whole thing work.</p><a class="quiet-link" href={local('work/')}>See that in my projects <Arrow /></a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="about-approach" id="experience" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
    <div class="about-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2 id="approach-title">From the parts<br />to the whole.</h2></div>
    <div class="approach-notes">
      <article>
        <span class="approach-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Start with what the system needs to do.</h3>
          <p>For EV maintenance, I started by asking which messages matter most when the network is busy. That helped me design an experiment that tracked routine diagnostics, alerts, and fault reports separately and measured delivery alongside latency.</p>
          <a class="quiet-link" href={local('work/ev-connectivity-qos/')}>EV telemetry experiment <Arrow /></a>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="approach-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Follow the connections.</h3>
          <p>In my 5G Standalone lab, the device could register and start a session but received no replies. I followed its traffic out of the 5G core, found the missing address translation rule, and confirmed the fix by checking that replies came back.</p>
          <a class="quiet-link" href={local('work/5g-sa-validation/')}>5G fault diagnosis <Arrow /></a>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="approach-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Make the work understandable.</h3>
          <p>In the Kubernetes 5G lab, I used separate checks for workload readiness and restored user traffic. I saved the dashboard and test procedure so someone else could see why a workload marked ready still needed help to restore service.</p>
          <a class="quiet-link" href={local('work/cloud-native-5g/')}>5G service recovery tests <Arrow /></a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="about-perspectives" aria-label="Research and teaching">
    <article id="education"><p class="eyebrow">A broader view</p><h2>Electronics, in context.</h2><p>My bachelor’s degree at E-JUST gave me a foundation in electronics and communications. My master’s in Industrial Engineering at Polytechnique Montréal brought that into the context of maintenance and reliability: understanding what a communication system needs to support in the real world.</p><a class="quiet-link" href={local('work/ev-connectivity-qos/')}>The EV maintenance project <Arrow /></a></article>
    <article id="teaching"><p class="eyebrow">Learning through teaching</p><h2>Making ideas click.</h2><p>Teaching has been another way to work through difficult ideas. At E-JUST, I supported courses and labs across circuits, signals, communications, and mathematics. Later, I co-lectured in fault analysis and maintenance at Polytechnique.</p><p>I enjoy the step between a concept on the board and something you can measure, test, or explain in your own words.</p><a class="quiet-link" href={local('resume/#teaching')}>Teaching experience and courses <Arrow /></a></article>
  </section>
  <section class="about-personal" id="leadership" aria-labelledby="personal-title">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Beyond the technical work</p><h2 id="personal-title">There’s more<br />to the picture.</h2><p>University was also about people. Through the student association and the operations team at E-JUST, I helped represent students, organize events, and bring campus initiatives together.</p><p>Outside work, I paint, listen to music, watch films, and take photos. The other side of this site is a small collection of those things.</p><a class="text-link" href={local('outside/')}>Step outside work <Arrow /></a></div>
    <a class="about-art" href={local('outside/#paintings')} aria-label="Explore my paintings"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset={local('images/personal/triptych-1200.avif')} /><img src={local('images/personal/triptych-1200.webp')} width="1536" height="1024" alt="Three of my abstract canvas panels displayed on a sunlit wall." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></picture></a>
  </section>
  <div class="about-record"><div><p class="eyebrow">Looking for the details?</p><p>The full record of my experience, education, publications, and recognition is on my resume.</p></div><a class="text-link" href={local('resume/')}>View my resume <Arrow /></a></div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .approach-notes a { margin-top: 10px; font-size: 13px; }
</style>
